// Regression test: this is the literal bug report. Before this fix,
// Resolver::visitFuncCall resolved ANY bare (unqualified) function call by
// searching EVERY module ever transitively imported anywhere in the
// program (SymTable_.findInModules(CalleeName), with no check that the
// CURRENT file itself imported -- let alone asked to expose unqualified --
// that specific module). Confirmed live: bare `printline("x");` compiled
// successfully with ZERO import statements anywhere in user code, because
// lib/std/prelude.tr always `import io;`-ed for every compile and this
// lookup didn't care who actually requested that visibility.
//
// A bare call must now resolve ONLY to (a) a function declared directly in
// the current file, or (b) -- if the bare call happens INSIDE a module's
// own body -- another function declared in that SAME module (self-
// reference, see selfref_module.tr/selfref_module_call.tr). Calling an
// imported module's function bare from OUTSIDE that module, like here,
// must be a clean UnknownFunctionError telling you to qualify it, not a
// silent cross-module resolution.

import cross_module_bare_call_module;

fn main(): int {
    mut x: int = helperFn();
    return 0;
}
